.TH SETUP_OPENWEBUI 1 "2026-08-12" "ragfarm" "ragfarm agent layer"
.SH NAME
setup_openwebui.py \- reproducible Open WebUI configuration: model presets, tool servers, prompts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B .venv/bin/python infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
.RB [ --only-active ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Open WebUI keeps its configuration in a database inside a Docker volume. That
makes it durable and unreviewable. This script is the
.B source
of that configuration: everything the UI needs in order to be useful \- which
models appear, what system prompt each carries, which tools each may call, what
sampler it uses \- is declared here and written over the API.
.PP
It is idempotent. Run it as often as you like; it converges rather than
duplicating. It is the reason a fresh Open WebUI volume can be brought back to a
working state without anyone clicking through the admin panel.
.PP
It runs
.B automatically
after every
.BR activate_llm (1)
activation and every
.BR --clear .
You run it by hand when the picker disagrees with reality, after Open WebUI has
been recreated, or after editing anything in
.BR MODEL_TUNING .
.SS What one run does
.IP 1. 4
Checks the draw.io viewer is reachable at the baked host and warns if
.B RAGFARM_PUBLIC_HOST
was autodetected rather than configured.
.IP 2. 4
Registers the mcpo tool servers.
.IP 3. 4
Points Open WebUI at every live vLLM slot. This must go through the API, not
compose: OWUI seeds
.B OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS
from the environment on
.B first start only
and then persists it, so a compose change alone leaves an existing deployment on
one endpoint and the second model silently never appears.
.IP 4. 4
Installs the
.B reboot_guarded
Python tool.
.IP 5. 4
Upserts one preset per live slot, from the registry and the tuning table.
.IP 6. 4
Prunes presets it previously created whose model is no longer served.
.SH THE ONE PLACE TO EDIT
.B MODEL_TUNING
near the top of the file. It has two halves:
.TP
.B models
Keyed by model
.B alias
\- the id the inference server advertises on
.IR /v1/models .
Legacy entries; the registry supersedes this for anything added since.
.TP
.B profiles
Keyed by the registry's
.B profile
field:
.B vision-thinking
and
.BR vision-instruct .
Each supplies a prompt body, sampler parameters and a capability matrix. This is
what a model added by
.BR fetch_llm (1)
selects with
.BR --profile .
.PP
Only differences from the
.BR params_common / caps_common
blocks belong in an entry. Nothing below the table needs touching to add a model.
.SH PROMPT BODIES
.B PROMPT_BODIES
maps a name to the system prompt text. Three exist:
.TP
.B text
The old text-only preset. Historical.
.TP
.B vision
The current prompt, RULE 0 through RULE 6. Used by
.BR vision-thinking .
.TP
.B vision-legacy
The July prompt, loaded from
.IR infra/openwebui/prompts/ .
Used by
.B vision-instruct
since 2026-08-12.
.PP
The two vision bodies diverged deliberately. The August prompt work was written
against and measured on a Thinking checkpoint and nearly doubled the prompt;
Instruct, which has no reasoning pass to absorb that much instruction, answered
better on the shorter one.
.B Consequence:
Instruct versus Thinking is no longer a single-variable comparison. Point both
profiles at
.B vision
before running any A/B.
.SH WHY RE-RUNNING IS NOT OPTIONAL
Presets bind to a model
.B by alias.
Change what a slot serves without re-running this, and the preset names an alias
that no longer exists. The picker then offers only the
.B raw
base models \- no system prompt, no tools, no grounding rules. Nothing looks
broken; the answers are just silently ungrounded. Observed on 2026-08-07 after
switching both slots from NVFP4 to FP8, which is why
.BR activate_llm (1)
now calls this itself.
.SH PRESET PRUNING
A preset is a row in Open WebUI's database, not a view of the registry, so every
model swap leaves the previous one behind \- still listed, still selectable, and
failing at the first message because its base model is not served. Four had
accumulated against two live ones by 2026-08-12.
.PP
The prune is deliberately narrow. Only ids this script is known to have created
are candidates: the
.B preset
field of every registry entry, plus the
.B preset_id
values in
.BR MODEL_TUNING["models"] .
A preset made by hand in the UI is
.B never
touched, however dead it looks \- guessing wrong there destroys someone's work,
while leaving a stale preset only makes the picker confusing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OWUI_URL
Default
.IR http://127.0.0.1:3000 .
.TP
.BR OWUI_TOKEN " | " OWUI_EMAIL " + " OWUI_PASSWORD
Admin credentials. One or the other is required; the script exits immediately
without them. Both live in
.IR .env ,
so
.B "source scripts/proxy-env.sh"
first.
.TP
.B RAGFARM_PUBLIC_HOST
Baked into the draw.io wrapper in RULE 5 \- the one place in the system where
127.0.0.1 is wrong, because a remote browser fetches it. See
.BR env (1).
.TP
.BR MCPO_RAG_URL ", " MCPO_PLACEMENT_URL
Tool server endpoints.
.TP
.BR TEXT_BASE_MODEL_ID ", " VISION_BASE_MODEL_ID
Optional pins that force an alias instead of autodetecting from
.IR /v1/models .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --only-active
Write just the preset for the currently served model, rather than one per slot.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Normal use
.RS
.nf
source scripts/proxy-env.sh
\&.venv/bin/python infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
.fi
.RE
.SS The picker is empty or a preset says "Model not found"
Usually means the slot was not serving when the preset was written, or the model
is genuinely down. Check the stack first, then re-run:
.RS
.nf
scripts/stack.sh status
source scripts/proxy-env.sh
\&.venv/bin/python infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
The script, and the tuning table inside it.
.TP
.I infra/openwebui/prompts/
Prompt bodies kept as files rather than Python literals.
.TP
.I infra/openwebui/tools/reboot_guarded.py
The confirmation-gated reboot tool.
.TP
.I models/llm/active.json
Supplies identity: alias, preset id, display name, profile. See
.BR active.json (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR activate_llm (1),
.BR stack (1),
.BR active.json (1),
.BR fetch_llm (1),
.BR env (1)
.PP
.I docs/decisions/ADR-0003
for the agent layer;
.I docs/deployment.md
for the demo surface these presets expose.
